#
# Degrade the lidar stream at the driver/transport boundary.
#
# Served as /faults/set_scan_degradation by ral_faults/fault_manager.py, which
# applies it by calling scan_fault_relay's own
# /scan_fault_relay/set_degradation with this same request. The relay remains
# the injection point and the SOLE publisher of /scan; the manager serves the
# /faults/* name because one node has to own the capability gate, the
# FaultEvent stream and /faults/active, and splitting that ownership would let
# two nodes disagree about which faults are in force.
#
# Injection point: scan_fault_relay, the SOLE publisher of /scan - so AMCL and
# both costmaps are downstream of this by construction, and the undegraded
# stream stays visible on /scan_raw for comparison.
#
# `enabled` false restores the faithful pass-through; the remaining fields are
# then ignored, and are therefore not range-checked on a disable. Disabling
# when nothing is in force is a FAILURE, not a no-op, for the same reason
# ClearObstacle's is. A disable publishes `cleared` for the fault it removed
# and NO event of its own: this kind is a singleton, so the disable is not
# itself something in force, and an `activated` for it would leave a reader
# computing "in force = activated - cleared" with a permanent phantom. The
# disable's id appears in that `cleared` event's detail and in the service
# response.
#
# `range_noise_stddev_m` is additive zero-mean Gaussian noise per beam.
# `max_range_m` truncates returns beyond it to the scan's own range_max+ (i.e.
# reports them as no-return), modelling a lidar that has lost reach.
# `blind_sector_start_deg`/`blind_sector_end_deg` blank an angular sector,
# measured in the scan's own frame, counter-clockwise from start to end; when
# start > end the sector wraps through +-180 deg.
# `dropout_probability` is the per-beam chance of a no-return, drawn from the
# RNG seeded by the scenario's `seed`.
#
# On an enable, every one of those five is checked against
# ral_faults/config/fault_catalogue.yaml's scan_degradation.param_ranges and
# refused when outside it or non-finite - `max_range_m: 0.0`, the value a
# partially filled request carries, would otherwise blank every beam and be
# reported as a successful activation.
#
# `fault_id` is the join key for this fault instance: the value the caller will
# see again on every FaultEvent the manager publishes for it. The scenario
# runner sends the id it minted (<kind>_<1-based index after sorting by
# at_sec>, scenario.py:308). An EMPTY string means "allocate one", which is
# what the dashboard sends; the manager answers with <kind>_ui_<n>. The
# RESPONSE carries the id the manager used - the one sent, or the minted one
# when the request left it empty.
#
# `success` is true only once the new parameters are in force in the relay.

string fault_id
bool enabled
float32 range_noise_stddev_m
float32 max_range_m
float32 blind_sector_start_deg
float32 blind_sector_end_deg
float32 dropout_probability
---
string fault_id
bool success
string message
